      "title": "HTTP URL Encoded: Keys and values are not FT_STRINGZ",
      "message": "HTTP URL Encoded: Keys and values are not FT_STRINGZ\n\nThe keys and values in HTTP form URL encoded are not null terminated\nin the packet, so they are not FT_STRINGZ.\n\nThis doesn't in practice affect anything right now, but it might make\nsense in the future to use a length of 0 for FT_STRINGZ to mean\n\"unknown, search for the terminator\" in the same way that -1 does now,\nin order to allow switching to unsigned lengths. A real FT_STRINGZ\nshould never have length zero. If a protocol has \"a null terminated\nstring, but also it can be completely missing\" there should be some\nother way to determine that it is missing, as searching for the NUL\nwill cause an exception. That other method likely means that the field\nshould really be a FT_STRINGZTRUNC or FT_STRINGZPAD instead.\n",
